Fresh-eyes review complete on `src/blog/people/drafts/Freddie-Mercury.md`. The notes are appended as a `FRESH EYES REVIEW (2026-08-28)` comment block at the bottom of the file — no body edits made, per the command.

The two biggest issues I flagged:

1. **The music is missing.** "Bohemian Rhapsody" exists only as a streaming statistic, and the Rabbit Hole's claim that his "catalog marinates in loss" is never demonstrated in an actual song. For a reader who arrived through the movie, that's the most conspicuous absence — and it's the one place the Type 4 frame could touch the art itself.

2. **The identity thesis never closes its loop.** The draft opens with Farrokh → Freddie as a one-way construction ("never went back to Farrokh") but skips the payoff that his funeral was conducted with Zoroastrian rites — the last ceremony of his life happened in the identity he left at eight. One sentence before the ashes beat would resolve what the childhood section sets up.

Third-tier but worth acting on: the 1985 Wigg interview powers six of nine sections, with the scars quote and the returned-gifts anecdote each appearing three-plus times — and the body is already at the ~4,500-word lint ceiling, so I noted that those echoes should be trimmed *before* any of the expansion material goes in. I also flagged what to protect: the teeth/hand-over-mouth open, the "Scene by Scene" court-exhibit section, the Freestone "loved laughing" counterweight, and the ashes-vs-gala ending.
